{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>When you come into your fellow's vineyard.</b> It is juxtaposed here to tell you that in what is your own, the matter of a vow applies, and in what belongs to others, it is apt to say 'you shall not take.'" "<b>And you shall eat grapes according to your appetite.</b> According to the appetite of the laborer — just as your appetite: if you muzzled, you are exempt, so too a laborer: if he muzzled, he is exempt. Each commandment stands on its own, and the juxtapositions are by way of derashah, as Rav Yosef said: even according to the one who does not derive juxtapositions throughout the entire Torah, in Mishneh Torah he does derive them. He warned the poor man not to rob grapes, and a sickle among standing grain and take a wife and she becomes his, and also the other — and she goes out from his domain; and one who takes a wife should rejoice with her and not be distressed to go out in the army; and a millstone upper and lower is oppression; and also one who kidnaps a person; and likewise the plague of tzara'at — that if they do not keep the laws, it harms those nearby; and likewise 'you shall not oppress the poor man in his pledge'; and the hired laborer; and the small children who die for their father's sin; and the perversion of justice for a stranger, orphan, and widow; and seizing a widow's garment; and the forgotten sheaf in the field for the poor; and likewise 'you shall not mistreat'; and likewise 'lest he add and your fellow be degraded'; and muzzling the ox is oppression — he labors and does not eat; and one who refuses levirate marriage is like oppression; and the seizure of the genitals is oppression; stone and stone, ephah and ephah is outright oppression; also Amalek committed oppression against Israel."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:54:06.170232101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}